A||||20||Darren's 87 coupe||car's weight - the cradle=???lbs||||1||
Z||000000||Darren's 87 coupe||09-19-2001||10:40 AM||||I plan on dropping the engine cradle and all out the bottom in the next month or two and was wondering what the weight of the car is when I lift it?  With the cradle on the floor that will be a lot of weight i won't have to lift. Any guesses?  It's a 87 coupe few options 2.5 w/air 5 speed mostly stock everything.  any guesses would be helpful.<BR>Darren||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||09-19-2001||10:50 AM||||I would venture to say -under 500lbs<BR>Two (big) guys could lift it. I've hooked a hoist to the rafter in the garage and lifted the cradleless body with nary a rafter creak or groan||||reg||1
Z||000002||Darren's 87 coupe||09-19-2001||11:50 AM||||wow phil that's a lot lighter than i expected.  That makes me feel a little better about doing it at home in my garage.  Thanks for the guesstimation<BR>Darren||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||09-19-2001||12:26 PM||||someone mentioned that without the engine/cradle they were able to lift the body by the trunk latch hook. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||09-19-2001||03:37 PM||||I would say closer to 2000lbs without the cradle for the whole car, but since you are lifting only the rear (I assume), under 1000 lbs.<P>No way I'd believe it's near 500 lbs to lift the rear.<P>Doesn't mean it's not, but don't use a lifting device rated that low.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||09-19-2001||05:01 PM||||I removed the rear bumper and stuck a bar thru the bumper support and lifted it from there (with a hoist). My brother and I were able to grab the bar and lift the rear of the car off the jack stands, not for long but we were able to do it||||reg||1
Z||000006||KRMFiero||09-19-2001||07:55 PM||||Maybe just how it was but when i had the 4X4 under the middle of the car and the car was jacked up about 3' from ground with no cradle i was able to lift it up about a 2 inches (the back of car) so someone could get a 2x4 ontop of the 4x4.... maybe it was just how it was positioned   who knows ||||reg||1
Z||000007||artherd||09-19-2001||08:19 PM||||daaamn, you're huge   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>In all seriousness, there is not a good deal of weight left with the cradle out, but don't bet on it being less than a thousand pounds (infact, don't bet on it being less than FIVE thousand pounts, just in case   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/northstar/drop-cradle.jpg">  <P>Best!!<BR>Ben.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KRMFiero:<BR><B>Maybe just how it was but when i had the 4X4 under the middle of the car and the car was jacked up about 3' from ground with no cradle i was able to lift it up about a 2 inches (the back of car) so someone could get a 2x4 ontop of the 4x4.... maybe it was just how it was positioned   who knows </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">   <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"<p>[This message has been edited by artherd (edited 09-19-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000008||Turbo88||09-19-2001||08:30 PM||||How in the hell did you get that little white fiero up on that stack of 4x4s <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">???||||reg||1
Z||000009||LS1swap||09-19-2001||09:06 PM||||I use a 6"x8" wood beam and a 1.5 ton hoist. Any rental store should have one if you don't want to buy one (mine cost about $80). If you pull the carpet back on the engine side of the trunk you will see a brace that ties the two strut towers together, in them there are two oval holes I  place the hooks to a 3/8" chain in them then hook the hoist to that. I would guess the back of the car without the cradle weighs about 500 to 700 lb. I would not try to lift it by the trunk latch. you may also want to attach four casters to the bottom of the cradle once you get it out. This makes it much easier to position for reinstalling the engine. You can see one of the casters in the picture I am including. I wish I had a picture of my hoist set up I don't.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/18_15_4.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||BlackDragon||09-19-2001||09:20 PM||||arthered, is that setup safe for the underside of a fiero? just wondering. im rebuilding an engine right now, and next weekend i will have to drop the cradle, and i need info on it too<P>------------------<BR>We are all different for a reason<P><BR>BlackDragon||||reg||1
Z||000011||Turbo88||09-20-2001||11:01 AM||||Weekend after next, the engine/trans is coming out of my 88 Formula. I have to replace the front exhaust manifold, and put in an new transaxle. Anyone have any tips or tricks? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Wannabe||09-20-2001||11:14 AM||||Is that LS1 mounted to the cradle, or just sitting on it? Is this really being done? All I've heard so far about LS1 swaps have been rumblings, but no facts/results/problems etc.||||reg||1
Z||000013||MinnGreenGT||09-20-2001||11:28 AM||||I bump the question to LS1 Swap... is that an LS1?  I too have only heard rumors!  Details!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/halfpole.gif">  <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000014||skitime||09-20-2001||11:51 AM||||Just a side note to this discussion.  I was talking to an old retired Pontaic mechanic a while ago.  He said that they would drop the engine and have four guys lift the car up and walk the car forward and set it back down clear of the engine.  If this is true, then the car cannot be very heavy without the cradle and engine.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||LS1swap||09-20-2001||09:13 PM||||In that picture it is about half and half. The picture was taken when I was fabricating engine mounts, and working on the starter drive. The picture is about a month old. I have since completed both. But lets not get carried away I still have a ways to go. I also did a test fit that weekend here are a few of  pics. I have had e few hurdles to over come. The flywheel and the starter drive being the hardest two, the front pulley wasn't exactly easy (it is also done but again I don't have any resent picture) I wish I had a digital camera but I don't so I just take pictures as I go and when a roll is full I take it in for a picture disk.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/19_16.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/21_18.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/25_22.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Banzai||09-21-2001||09:14 AM||||I took mine out about a month ago. After having to cut the front cradle bolts, (AHHHHHH, the bushing sleeves were frozen to the bolts) we placed a 4x4 at the rear of the body just before the engine bay(as illustrated in the Chilton and Haynes manuals)and put a floor jack under the post. Since I was replacing the motor and tranny mounts(about $25 for all) I just hooked a hoist to the motor and lowered it onto dolly(bottom part of a 1200lb. capacity motor stand) then jacked the body up until it cleared. Went pretty smoothly.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000017||BigTurbo87GT||09-21-2001||11:51 AM||||Sorry for hijacking the thread with LS1 questions but ...<BR>LS1Swap: Does the LS1 Intake bolt in with throttle body facing forward OR backward?<BR>I'm doing a Northstar swap at the moment but I'm thinking the next will be an LS1. Who made your flywheel for you? It looks like it's aluminum.||||reg||1
Z||000018||LS1swap||09-21-2001||08:07 PM||||An LS1 is unique in which the intake is reversible for it only attaches to the symmetrical heads and not to the valley. The only modification I had to do to accomplish it was to make an adapter for the oil pressure sender(16mmx1.5) which would hit the throttle body if not modified. Keep in mind you only revers the intake, the wiring to the injectors has to remain the same. the flywheel is a fadanza flywheel. It is aluminum, I had an aluminum ring machined to keep the friction surface in the correct location. It was made to fit in the recess is which the original steel friction surface was inserted. I wish I had more pictures but I don't, but trust me I will when I am done I was just trying to show the guy where to install casters for reinstalling his engine. the casters do make it much easier to reinstall an engine. Don't get me wrong I will be happy to answer any questions that I can it is just that I am only about 90% complete so there is about 10 % that I feel I know how I am going to do, but want to Finnish before I comment on them. No offense to anyone but there are so many people that speak before they know around here, and I do not want to be among them. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||artherd||09-22-2001||12:04 AM||||Hi there!!<P>The car is resting on it's jack points, and is *very* secure (heh) however, positioning is critical, you have to put both beams directly under the jack points, or else it could be imbalanced, or damage the underside.  The car sits on the frame rails themselves, and dosen't crush anything, if you did it right  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>(update: I was able to drop the fuel tank with this rig, which has a hole in back cut in to the timbers.  Piece of cake, took me 15min.)<P>PS: the Northstar's intake is similar to the LS1 in that you can 'reverse' it.  Pretty cool, and nice of GM  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  (though the Northstar is already facing the right way for us 'FWD' layout transverse cars, hotrodders like it because it can be easially reversed to a 'RWD' arrangement)<P>That LS1 swap looks pretty sweet, and WOW that's a flywheel and a half!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackDragon:<BR><B>arthered, is that setup safe for the underside of a fiero? just wondering. im rebuilding an engine right now, and next weekend i will have to drop the cradle, and i need info on it too<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000020||Leper||09-22-2001||01:41 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/northstar/drop-cradle.jpg"> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have to say, that that, is the coolest fort I've ever seen  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>Darren, about 55% or so of the weight of a Fiero is over the back wheels, I'd guess that the parts that make up most of that are bolted to the cradle so taking the cradle out would remove about 1/2 the weight from the car. The car also gets lighter since you have to drain the coolant and remove a bunch of parts just to get to the point of taking the cradle out. Then most of the weight is supported by the front wheels, making the back easy to lift. Not to mention<BR>that while the back is in the air, even more of the weight will transfer to the front, explaining how KRM was able to lift the car by himself. (Or he's just really really strong.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<BR>||||reg||1
